@@s001 DP on trees and DAGs: the pyramid
@@s002 dato:value of the cell,ora:OPT computed,ok:chosen path
@@s003 Pyramid: from each cell you go down to the left or to the right. It is a DAG: no cycles, so there is a natural order of computation, from the bottom up.
@@s004 Last row: OPT = value of the cell. The three cells at the bottom are worth 2, 0, 0: OPT = 2, 0, 0.
@@s005 Row above: OPT = value + max(OPT of the children). For the 2 on the right: 2 + max(0, 0) = 2. For the cell 1 on the left: 1 + max(2, 0) = 3.
@@s006 At the top: 1 + max(3, 2) = <b>4</b>. For the path go down to the child with the greater OPT: left, then left. Value 1 + 1 + 2 = 4.
@@s007 DP on trees and DAGs: the pyramid
@@s008 When the path can be followed in only one direction (a pyramid, a DAG, a tree), the DP computes the best value from the bottom up. It is the Tower problem of the exam.
@@s009 The problem
@@s010 A pyramid of cells with numbers. From each cell you go down to the left or to the right until the bottom: you want the path with the maximum sum. There are no cycles, because you always go down: it is a DAG.
@@s011 From the bottom up: OPT of a cell
@@s012 The recurrence
@@s013 <code>OPT(i, j) = value(i, j) + max( OPT(i+1, j), OPT(i+1, j+1) )</code>, with the last row as base case. The answer is <code>OPT(0, 0)</code>. For the path: always go down to the child with the greater OPT.
@@s014 Cost and general form
@@s015 Pyramid of <code>N</code> levels: <code>O(N²)</code> time and space. Generic DAG: <code>O(n + m)</code>, computing in reverse topological order. The same form is Bellman-Ford: the counter of edges is the "row" of the table.
@@s016 The code, side by side
@@s017 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
@@s018 <a href="../codice/dp-dag-piramide.html"><b>→ Java code for this topic</b></a>
@@s019 Longest path in a DAG
@@s020 Points on a mountain, connected by slopes. Each slope goes from <code>u</code> to <code>v</code> only downhill and has a score <code>c</code>. You can start and stop wherever you like. You want the path with the <b>highest sum of scores</b>.
@@s021 Three sentences of the text decide everything:
@@s022 «start and end … at any point»: no fixed start. Each point can be the beginning, with value 0.
@@s023 «maximum sum»: the <i>longest</i> path, not the shortest.
@@s024 «all slopes go downhill» (no cycles): the graph is a <b>DAG</b> (<i>directed acyclic graph</i>, a directed graph without cycles).
@@s025 The longest path in any graph is NP-hard (you will see it with the Hamiltonian path). On a DAG instead it is dynamic programming in polynomial time: the absence of cycles allows a computation order, the <a href="topological-sort.html">topological</a> one.
@@s026 I define <code>OPT(i, v)</code> = the score of the best path that arrives at <code>v</code> with <b>at most</b> <code>i</code> slopes.
@@s027 <code>OPT(0, v) = 0</code> for every <code>v</code>: without slopes, the helicopter left you at <code>v</code>.
@@s028 <code>OPT(i, v) = max( OPT(i−1, v), max over each slope (u, v, c) of OPT(i−1, u) + c )</code>
@@s029 The first term is "I do not use a last slope". The second is "I arrive at <code>v</code> with one more slope, from the point <code>u</code>". Answer: the maximum over <code>v</code> of <code>OPT(n−1, v)</code>, because a path with <code>n</code> points uses at most <code>n − 1</code> slopes. Time O(n·m), space O(n²) with the whole table.
